h NATO was  expanded to include Eastern European  nations healing the rift that 60 years  earlier had torn apart her native land  according to Ralph Waldo Emerson there  is no history only biographies and how  much more revealing is an autobiography  a memoir bringing to life not only the  experiences but the private thoughts and  feelings of the famous and the powerful  Madam Secretary explores Madeleine  Albright's childhood and immigration her  more traditional role as wife and mother  of three daughters her painful divorce  and her fascinating years in politics  she gives us lively candid impressions  of slobodan milosevic Ariel Sharon  Yasser Arafat and President Clinton  indeed her years in office were pivotal  and saw the horrors of Kosovo the  daunting challenges of bringing peace to  the Middle East the defining of a  post-cold war relationship with Russia  and NAT last but not least Monica  Lewinsky throughout most of her career  secretary Albright found herself the  only woman at the table and as such had  to work extra hard to make her voice  heard we are delighted to have her here  with us today and as I mentioned Sam  Donaldson will be conducting the  interview Sam a familiar face in every  American's living room has been with ABC  News for 36 years has served two  appointments as chief White House  correspondent and currently hosts the  Sam Donaldson show live in America a  daily news talk radio program Sam we are  honored to have you with us today  Dean Acheson once observed that the  prerequisite to be Secretary of State  was to be boring where did you go wrong  well I decided that it was much more fun  to be myself and tell it like it is and  be a woman and do it my way well of  course the fact that you're here is  amazing your name wasn't Madeline was  mad Lanka and you were born in what was  then czechoslovakia and and your parents  had to flee what the Nazis to begin with  yes and I actually was my name was Maria  Jana which is Mary Jane but my 